; model_copy_price_admitted: the same model copy, at exactly the budget it
; costs.
;
; The upper half of the pair model_copy_price_refused opens. That vector
; refuses the copy at 127 bytes; this one admits the whole program at 136,
; so between them the model copy price is pinned to a number rather than
; bounded on one side. `expected.json` has no bytes field -- a vector
; asserts a fault or an outcome -- so a boundary pair is how a charge is
; pinned across implementations.
;
; Sizing. BQMX of size 4 charges 32, SETLINE charges one linear entry at
; 32, and OUTPUT charges model_bytes = 4 * 8 + 32 = 64, which is the 128
; the refused vector sits one byte under. The trailing int write adds one
; more copy at the variable rate, 8, for 136.
;
; That trailing write is not decoration. OUTPUT leaves a model in output
; slot 0, and the conformance format renders a non-scalar output as a
; sentinel rather than a value, so a vector that halted there would assert
; nothing useful about what it produced. Overwriting slot 0 with an int
; leaves a slot the format can state, while the model copy it is pinning
; has already been charged.
;
; Why this path rather than ITER over a vec<xqmx>, which is the other
; opcode the one-price rule names: no instruction appends a model to a
; vec. VECX creates an empty vec<xqmx> and VECPUSH takes an int, so a
; populated vec<xqmx> is reachable only by host injection and cannot be
; expressed as bytecode plus calldata. The Rust suite pins ITER against
; OUTPUT directly (iterating_a_model_vec_costs_what_outputting_the_same_
; model_costs); this pair pins OUTPUT across implementations, and the two
; together carry the rule to both.

PUSH 4
BQMX r0          ; charges 4 * VARIABLE_BYTES = 32

PUSH 0
PUSH 1
SETLINE r0       ; charges LINEAR_ENTRY_BYTES = 32, total 64

PUSH 0
OUTPUT r0        ; charges model_bytes = 64, total 128

PUSH 5
STOW r1
PUSH 0
OUTPUT r1        ; charges VARIABLE_BYTES = 8, total 136
HALT
